WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.380
Light an oat seedling from one side, and its shoot bends toward the lamp.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.140
Auxin, a plant hormone, moves toward the shaded flank.

3
00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.440
That side elongates faster, so the shoot bends toward the light.

4
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.080
Gravity also redirects auxin.

5
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:22.680
It collects on the lower side, making a shoot curve up and a root curve down.

6
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.300
The plant steers its growth by moving a chemical signal.

7
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.720
Unequal growth on opposite sides makes the bend.

8
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:32.573
How does auxin change direction?

9
00:00:33.473 --> 00:00:38.653
In Arabidopsis hypocotyls, the seedling stem below the seed leaves, light changes

10
00:00:38.653 --> 00:00:41.773
where a carrier called PIN3 sits in the cell membrane.

11
00:00:42.773 --> 00:00:45.673
Many PIN carriers sit mainly on one face of a cell.

12
00:00:46.573 --> 00:00:48.153
Repositioning them steers auxin.

13
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:52.033
A blue-light receptor called phototropin detects the light.

14
00:00:53.017 --> 00:00:54.737
Now lay a seedling on its side.

15
00:00:55.617 --> 00:01:00.917
In Arabidopsis root-cap cells, PIN3 relocates to the new lower face, redirecting

16
00:01:00.917 --> 00:01:05.977
auxin downward. Extra auxin slows elongation on the lower side of the root.

17
00:01:06.737 --> 00:01:09.677
The upper side grows faster, so the root curves down.

18
00:01:10.557 --> 00:01:15.297
In the shoot, extra auxin speeds elongation on the lower side, so the shoot curves

19
00:01:15.297 --> 00:01:16.297
up.

20
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.420
The same hormone can act differently in different organs.

21
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.220
Plant cells make hormones that change growth, and the signals work together.

22
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.920
Auxin helps the growing shoot tip suppress side buds, together with cytokinins and

23
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.740
strigolactones. Other hormones control other transitions.

24
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.220
Ethylene promotes fruit ripening.

25
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.980
Tomatoes engineered to make little ethylene failed to ripen normally.

26
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.620
Supplied ethylene restored ripening.

27
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:46.513
Light also helps time flowering.

28
00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:49.833
Plants use light receptors and a daily clock.

29
00:01:50.713 --> 00:01:55.733
In Arabidopsis, a long-day plant, evening light stabilizes the protein C .O .N .S .D

30
00:01:55.733 --> 00:01:59.113
.N .S. When days are long enough, C .O .N .S .D .N .S.

31
00:01:59.273 --> 00:02:02.163
promotes the gene FT in leaf phloem.

32
00:02:02.163 --> 00:02:05.653
FT protein travels to the shoot apex, where flowers form.

33
00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.713
Leaves read the timing, and a mobile signal carries the message.

34
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:12.953
Hormones also have opposing effects.

35
00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:17.713
Abscisic acid maintains seed dormancy, a state of arrested growth.

36
00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:19.993
Gibberellins promote germination.

37
00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:25.413
Their effects point in opposite directions: keeping a seed dormant, or starting

38
00:02:25.413 --> 00:02:26.413
seedling growth.

39
00:02:27.117 --> 00:02:29.157
Hormones spread defence signals too.

40
00:02:30.217 --> 00:02:33.037
Jasmonates signal defence against plant-eating animals.

41
00:02:34.097 --> 00:02:39.417
Salicylic acid helps systemic acquired resistance: infection in one part leaves

42
00:02:39.417 --> 00:02:41.897
other parts more resistant to later attack.

43
00:02:42.777 --> 00:02:47.797
In tobacco, salicylic acid is needed in the distant protected leaves, but is not

44
00:02:47.797 --> 00:02:49.237
itself the travelling signal.

45
00:02:50.077 --> 00:02:55.137
Jasmonate for insects and salicylic acid for pathogens is a tendency, not a rule.
